  "causal_diagnosis": {
    "baseline_s21003": "At the nominal evidence boundary only front_right was not bearing; all four feet were bearing two ticks later and remained bearing. No timeout occurred. This is a measurement-acquisition defect under the already frozen 12-tick phase-skew and 3-tick minimum-airborne-dwell envelopes.",
    "rough_s21002": "front_right timed out at tick 1471 and front_left timed out at tick 1779, both at release phase 54 while still bearing after the unchanged 120-tick maximum. This is a release/unweighting defect.",
    "rough_s21001": "rear_right completed only one accepted cycle; three failed recontacts had relocation 0.005424, 0.004901, and -0.004946 m against 0.015 m, final lateral displacement was 0.100246 m against 0.10 m, and steering saturated 384 times. This is a path/foothold defect.",
    "observed_two_tick_delay_may_not_be_copied": true,
    "acquisition_window_derivation": "existing maximum contact-gated phase skew 12 ticks plus existing minimum airborne dwell 3 ticks, with that established temporal dwell reused prospectively as the all-support stability dwell",
    "controller_and_measurement_changes_separated": true
  },
  "mechanism_hypothesis": {
    "prospective_hypothesis": "A globally stronger contact-loaded release allowance can reduce rough-terrain contact-gate timeouts, while a smooth reciprocal steering-to-stride transform can prevent the current inner-stride collapse and improve path/foothold progress.",
